{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"flax-utensil","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"I'm setting up the new app, \"Vantage,\" on port 9770. The assertion model will include fields like id, at, subject, predicate, value, claimant, certainty, applicability, basis, supersedes, links, and tags. The ledger will handle appending validated assertions, querying with various filters, resolving the latest non-superseded assertion per subject/predicate based on effective confidence (considering source ceilings and user-set supremacy), and providing snapshot, partitioning, and compaction capabilities."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T08:52:33.594174500+00:00"}}}}
2026-11-23T08:52:34.768603Z  INFO chisel_agent::skills_loading: skills discovery: cwd=C:\builds\project workspace_root=C:\builds additional_dirs=1 providers=[devin,agents_standard,cursor,windsurf,claude,opencode,zed,copilot,codex,kiro] scanned_scopes=3 loaded=48 by_provider=[builtin=3,cursor=7,devin=38] errors=0
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"flax-utensil","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI'm refining how needs are managed. Instead of a separate in-memory store, I'll derive needs from ledger rows, specifically `need:open` assertions, and track their status through subsequent `need:*` rows. This simplifies state management by rebuilding a needs index from the ledger on demand. For templates, I'm defining slots, minimum certainty, and grant requirements. Invocation involves gathering candidates, evaluating gates (like missing required slots or failed acceptance tests), and then executing steps, ultimately fulfilling the need and recording results. Grants, like `publish.creator`, will be consumed during invocation; missing grants will defer the need."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T08:52:38.935082+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"flax-utensil","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI'm refining the expression evaluation, realizing that simple path resolution with \"$\" and template strings like \"{slot.name}\" is sufficient, rather than a more complex evaluator. Built-in tools will use Python handlers, while user-authored tools can use either handlers or a DSL for their steps. I'm also defining the structure for personas, including their attributes and how they're stored and ordered, and how new personas are \"minted\" with default confidence levels and generated attributes."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T08:52:42.817044200+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"flax-utensil","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI'm refining the ethics checks for figures. If a figure is modeled on a recognizable person, I'll perform a Wikipedia lookup. If the person is found and has died within the last 10 years or is still living, I'll require a mark and admonishment. The system will return the admonishment text, display a permanent mark (◆), and record these ethics gate rows. For composing or replying, I'll build a prompt including register constraints, a lens, and a fidelity rule. This involves gathering facts from relevant sources, using an LLM with a system prompt that emphasizes using only provided facts, and then performing a post-check for fidelity. If the response is ungrounded, I'll regenerate it once with stricter prompting; if it still fails, I'll withhold the response and flag it. A fallback path uses declared templates to ensure faithfulness."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T08:52:45.309907+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"flax-utensil","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI'm seeing that the system's registers are defined by their telegraphic, wry, warm, scholarly, catalogue, or measured styles, and that distinct system prompts lead to different outputs and fallback templates. The engines for LLM, TTS, STT, and Portrait are detailed, along with their dependencies and availability checks. The speech floor manages a queue for rendering and playing speech, measuring playback time, and logging admission and playback events. Connectors for Wikipedia, ICS, files, and RSS are also listed, specifying their data sources and retrieval methods."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T08:52:47.626022700+00:00"}}}}
